{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795556995,"nonce":"uh7FuYZ0z38fycbSDOuUgwOImPw=","title":"Every CI workflow builds the Zyntax compiler","message":"Reported by the owner on 2026-11-24. The DSL crates (blinc_dsl_core, blinc_cn_dsl, blinc_dsl_macro) and blinc_runtime depend on Zyntax (zyntax_embed, zyntax_typed_ast, zrtl, zynml, and through them Cranelift). No other workspace crate depends on them, but:\n\n- ci.yml Check, Clippy and Documentation ran `--workspace` with no excludes, so they built the DSL crates themselves;\n- the nextest and doctest runs (ci.yml on three OSes, coverage.yml, android.yml) excluded the three DSL crates but not blinc_runtime, which still pulled zyntax_embed;\n- android.yml's `cargo check --workspace` had no excludes.\n\nNot affected: msrv.yml (release crates only), ios-compile.yml and the docs workflow (single packages), the release build of blinc_cli.\n\nFix: each of the three workflows sets DSL_EXCLUDES once (the four crates) and uses it in every workspace command; the non-Linux test steps run in bash so it expands on Windows. A new dsl.yml checks and lints the four crates on ubuntu when their sources change, weekly (Monday 06:00 UTC) and on request. The trade-off: a change elsewhere that breaks the DSL crates is found by the weekly run, not on the push. Their tests stay out of CI, as before (known failures).\n\nVerified locally: `cargo tree` over the workspace with the four excludes has no zyntax, zrtl, zynml or cranelift crate; the excluded workspace check passes; the DSL check and clippy pass (warnings only, as in the main clippy job).","files":null}]}